# arcana-cadence.timer — look every minute, act on the interval the owner set.
#
# THE TIMER AND THE INTERVAL ARE DIFFERENT THINGS, and that split is right:
#
#   * The TIMER decides how often the system asks "is it time yet".
#   * The BINARY decides whether anything happens, by measuring the age of the
#     last recorded tick. Firing every minute cannot produce minute decisions.
#
# IT USED TO LOOK HOURLY, AND THAT WAS A BUG WORTH ONE HOUR EVERY TIME IT HIT.
#
# Observed on 2026-09-11. The last tick was at 05:00:31Z, so the four-hour
# cadence came due at 05:00:31Z. The timer fired at 05:00:30Z:
#
#   12:00:30 WIB  last decision was 4h0m0s ago; the cadence is 4h0m0s.
#                 Nothing to do (next at ~2026-09-11T05:00:31Z).
#
# One second short, and then nothing until the next hourly firing. A four-hour
# cadence checked hourly is a FIVE-hour cadence whenever it lands just before
# the threshold — and because each late tick moves the threshold later, the miss
# can repeat rather than self-correct.
#
# WHY EVERY MINUTE, and not five, or fifteen. The binary's floor is sixty
# seconds, set by the pool snapshot identifier, which has minute resolution. The
# rule is: THE TIMER MUST LOOK AT LEAST AS OFTEN AS THE SHORTEST INTERVAL THE
# BINARY WILL ACCEPT. Any coarser and an owner who chooses a one-minute cadence
# silently gets the timer's period instead, which is the same bug with a
# different number.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS. A run that is not due exits after three cheap reads — the
# competition, its open tick, and the last tick time — and `flock -n` means a
# run that overlaps a long one is skipped rather than queued. The cost of
# looking is small and fixed; the cost of not looking is an hour of an agent's
# cadence, every time it lands on the wrong side of a second.
#
# RandomizedDelaySec is DELIBERATELY SMALL here. It was 90 seconds, which was
# harmless against an hourly look and is nonsense against a one-minute one:
# jitter larger than the period makes the schedule meaningless.
#
# Persistent=true so a machine that was off through an interval checks on the
# next boot rather than waiting.
[Unit]
Description=Run the ARCANA continuous cadence check every minute

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* *:*:00 UTC
RandomizedDelaySec=5
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
